;; The enumeration, on a machine anyone can run.
;;
;; feature:board-demo is for a board on a bench: it prints what bound at the end
;; of boot, on the console, which is the one thing that works when nothing else
;; does. This image is the same thing under QEMU, for two reasons.
;;
;; It is how the script is tested at all. A shell script that walks sysfs and
;; joins two directory trees has edge cases - a node with no status, a device
;; bound to a bus that is itself a device, a symlink that does not resolve - and
;; none of them are visible until it runs against a real kernel. Under QEMU that
;; costs a minute rather than a card and a power cycle.
;;
;; And it is the reference output. A virt machine's tree is small and completely
;; known, so what this prints is what a working enumeration looks like. When a
;; real board prints something surprising, this is what to compare it against.
;;
;;   silt dt build/images/*.dtb --linux ~/linux     what should bind
;;   this image                                     what did
;;
;; Those two should agree, and QEMU is where that claim is cheap to check.

(image qemu-arm-board-demo
  (compose
    target:qemu-aarch64-virt
    profile:standard
    feature:networking
    feature:dev-ssh
    feature:board-demo)

  (ci boot)

  (verified-against (buildroot "2025.02.16")))
